In the coming decades, climate change will likely lead to environmental and economic disruption.\(^{1}\) Carbon dioxide, the primary greenhouse gas produced from the combustion of fossil fuels, is currently released into the atmosphere without mitigation at a rate of 36 Gton per year.\(^{2}\) Carbon capture and sequestration has been proposed to reduce the amount of atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). However, implementation of this technology necessitates low-cost and efficient methods for separating \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in a post-combustion capture process, where the carbon dioxide is removed from the effluent of power plants that burn coal or natural gas.\(^{3}\) One of the most energy-efficient ways of capturing \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is with a membrane-based separation.\(^{4}\) Among the design platforms available for gas separation membranes, mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs) are promising: MMMs offer the low cost, high processability, and mechanical properties of polymers and, if successful, the unbounded separation performance of porous materials. Many MMMs overcome the intrinsic permeability-selectivity tradeoff of pure polymers.\(^{5,6}\) In recent
years, zeolites and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been used as fillers in MMMs \(^{7-9}\) where,  typically,  molecular-sieving  and  solution-diffusion  mechanisms 10  promote  gas separation. In this work, we introduce for the first time a phase-change MOF 11—mmen\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) (mmen \(=N{,}N^{\prime}\) -dimethylethylenediamine; dobpdc \(\mathrm{\Delta^{4-}\,=\,4,4^{\circ}}\) -dioxidobiphenyl-3,3'- dicarboxylate—in a MMM to enhance the permeability and selectivity of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (Figure 1).

While the use of mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) as a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorbent and its associated thermodynamic underpinnings have been investigated thoroughly,\(^{11-16}\) the kinetics of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) transport in this phase-change material in a driven (i.e., out-of-equilibrium) system have not yet been reported; thus, their prospects for improving MMM-based gas separations are still unclear. It has been demonstrated that mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) has a novel cooperative \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake mechanism that leads to a non-classical, step-like adsorption isotherm.\(^{11}\) Having such a high, readily-reversible \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake makes the application of mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) in membranes very attractive. Until now, attempts to incorporate this phase-change MOF in a MMM have failed, likely due to the dimensions of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) crystals obtained through conventional synthetic approaches: as shown in Figure S1, high aspect-ratio microrods, when blended with polymer, typically protrude from the matrix, which also appear to exhibit poorly-formed polymer/MOF interfaces. To resolve issues associated with compositing larger MOF crystals, we developed a new synthesis of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals. In turn, diamines were grafted within these \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)-(dobpdc) nanocrystals to take advantage of their step-like \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorptive properties in a MMM for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separations. With these new diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals incorporated into MMMs, we find significant increases in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability while maintaining high \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity relative to the neat polymer matrix, essentially uncoupling conventional
permeability-selectivity trade-offs typified by all-polymer membranes. This result points to new opportunities for MOF nanocrystals to enhance the performance of MMMs \(^{9,17}\) and encourages further investigation of other phase-change adsorbents as active components in gas-separation membranes.

