Mixed-Matrix Membrane Phase-Change MOF Dynamic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) Uptake and Release

Figure 1. By incorporating phase-change MOFs in a mixed-matrix membrane, the permeability and selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separations can be greatly enhanced. Critical to the design of these composite membranes is the nanoscale dimension of the phase-change MOF filler, which serves to mitigate the effects of plasticization and likelihood of defects in the membrane that would negatively impact gas transport selectivity. Critical to the performance of the membranes is the dynamic uptake and release of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) along the diamine molecules appended to open metal sites lining the MOF channels.

To access \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals for MMM preparation, we developed a new synthetic procedure with the goal of obtaining dispersible nanocrystals with properties similar to bulk crystals (Figure 2). We were ultimately successful by introducing the \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) ligand to a super-stoichiometric amount of \(\mathrm{MgO}\) in DMF at \(120\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), \(^{18}\) which generates a coating of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods on \(\mathrm{MgO}\). Precursor stoichiometry and reaction time were varied in turn to
obtain \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods with controlled morphology and length (e.g., 100–200 nm in size) (Figure 2). To isolate MOF nanorods, the \(\mathrm{MgO}\) core was simply dissolved in acetic acid. The resulting purified MOF nanorods (Figure S3) were found to unaltered by the acetic acid treatment and they could be dispersed readily in polar aprotic solvents, such as DMF. Visualization of the reaction pathway is depicted in Figure S2. Notably, we found that higher Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas (Figure S4a) and higher amine loadings (Figure S4b), were achieved after treating the nanorods in a subsequent step with \(\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) in DMF at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), consistent with the presence of fewer defects in the final material.

![]('img_url')

Figure 2. Varying the ratio of \(\mathrm{MgO}\) to \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{dobpdc}\) ligand during the chemical synthesis of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) MOFs allows the reaction coordinate to diverge, yielding either a forest of nanocrystalline MOFs on \(\mathrm{MgO}\) solids or microcrystalline MOF rods. In the case of the former, the nanocrystalline MOFs can be released from the underlying \(\mathrm{MgO}\) solids using acetic acid as a
mild etchant. \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}\) byproducts are easily removed from the final nano \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) product.

Differences in the crystallinity of the \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods compared to the micron-scale rods obtained from a bulk preparation are evident in Figure 3. Both experimental diffraction patterns match the calculated reflections for the crystal structure, and the peak broadening observed for the nano-MOF sample is consistent with the expected Scherrer broadening. Using Scherrer analysis, we estimate the crystallite size to be \(\sim12\;\mathrm{nm}\), corresponding to the average nanorod cross-section. We further characterized the BET surface area for both samples (Figure S5), which unexpectedly revealed a greater surface area for the \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods than for the microrods. Deviations in behavior with regard to the shape of the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) isotherm manifests at high pressures, likely due to adsorption at surface sites, which are more prevalent in the nano-MOF.

![]('img_url')

Figure 3. (a) PXRD of \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanocrystals (red) and microcrystals (blue). In the nano-MOF sample, only high intensity peaks are visible and their FWHM is broader due to the small size of the crystallites. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms of nanocrystalline (red) and microcrystalline
(blue) mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) in either (b) linear or (c) semi-log plots. Saturation values are similar for both, while the adsorption step of mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) nanorods is broader.

To complete the preparation of the phase-change filler, \(N,N'\)-dimethylethylenediamine (mmen) molecules were grafted to the Lewis acidic open-metal sites within \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) in toluene. No activation procedure was employed prior to diamine grafting, as we found drying to be detrimental to achieving good nanorod dispersions in the polymer matrix. After activating both nano- and micron-scale samples of mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) MOFs at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), their \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake was evaluated. The resulting adsorption isotherms are shown in Figure 3 as linear and semi-log plots. The linear plot (Figure 3b) indicates \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) saturation capacity is quite comparable for the two crystal sizes, while the semi-log plot (Figure 3c) reveals that the adsorption "step" of the mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) nanorods is broader, corresponding to a lesser degree of cooperativity in the adsorption mechanism. This could be due to finite-size effects in the formation of carbamate chains that are limited by the nanocrystal length, or it could be the consequence of a higher number of mmen vacancies within the crystal. To put an upper bound on the latter, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out on nano- and micron-sized MOFs to quantify the mmen loadings. The results indicate a decrease in mmen loading within the nanorods of only \(1.4\%\) (Figure S6), which we believe to be negligible (i.e., within the experimental error).

To understand the influence of phase-change MOF fillers on membrane performance, we cast mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs) from a mixture of 6FDA-DAM polyimide and mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\)(dobpdc) nanorods in dichloromethane. The quality of the dispersion was excellent, yielding transparent, flexible membranes after solvent evaporation. These membranes were tested in a custom-built, constant-volume, variable-pressure, gas-permeation apparatus. Permeabilities of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)
and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) were recorded and selectivities were calculated from the acquired data at different input (upstream) pressures. Both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability (Figure 4a) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity (Figure 4b) are greater in the MMM than in the neat polymer. To better clarify the role of the diamines inside the MOF, we prepared a membrane with bare \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpcd})\) nanorods within 6FDA-DAM and tested \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) permeation under the same conditions. The results, plotted in Figure 4c, are consistent with previous reports that showed that nano \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\)/polymer MMMs outperform pure polymer membranes. \({}^{20}\) Figure 4c also shows that the diamines in mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpcd})\) nanorods significantly improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability while also positively affecting \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity (Table S1).

We further performed gas permeation measurements on both 6FDA-DAM and mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpcd})\)–6FDA-DAM membranes (Figure 57) operating at higher temperatures (35–75 \({}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). We noted that the decrease in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity with increasing temperature for the 6FDA-DAM membrane was significantly less pronounced than that for the mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpcd})\)–6FDA-DAM MMM. We expect that with increasing temperature, solubility selectivity is reduced in both the neat polymer and in the MMM, with solubility playing a more pronounced role in the adsorbent-loaded material. Considering the temperature-dependent step-like character of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and release that is the hallmark characteristic of the phase-change mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpcd})\) filler, adsorption sites within the MOF are left increasingly unoccupied at higher temperature, effectively decreasing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) solubility of the MMM. This effect dominates the steep decrease in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity with increasing temperature in the MMM, and corroborates our hypothesis that amine-appended MOFs primarily improve the performance of MMMs by increasing the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) solubility. These data also suggest that the prevalence of interfacial polymer–MOF voids in the MMM is minimal, or at least inconsequential.
Based on these results, we conclude that the diamines have an important role in the increase of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and that this is likely related to the unique \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) cooperative adsorption behavior. We further speculate that selectivities could increase in real operating conditions, e.g., in a mixed-gas environment. Indeed, under pure \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), the transport is unaffected by \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the pore and we may expect higher \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) permeability. Under a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) environment, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) can still transport down the channel while \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) may be excluded due to the carbamate formation that effectively reduce the pore volume. We note that, in absolute terms, other polymers (e.g., polymers of intrinsic microporosity or thermally-rearranged polymers) can achieve superior performance in terms of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability, although their lack of stability over time has been a serious concern, preventing their use in industrial applications.\(^{21,22}\) MMMs are generally less susceptible to aging, and MOF nanocrystal-polymer composite membranes are resistant to plasticization.\(^{17}\) Moreover, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption is not affected by humidity in the amine-appended adsorbents,\(^{21}\) unlike what is observed in metal–organic frameworks with bare metal sites.\(^{22,23}\)

