Figure 4. Gas permeability measurements on neat 6FDA-DAM polymer (black) and 6FDA-DAM mixed-matrix membranes (red) with loaded with \(23\%\) w/w mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods: (a) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability vs. upstream pressure; and (b) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity vs. upstream pressure. Both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity increase in the MMM compared to the neat
polymer. (c) Robeson plot for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separation showing performances of neat 6FDA-DAM (black), 6FDA-DAM mixed-matrix membranes incorporating \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods (green), and 6FDA-DAM mixed-matrix membranes incorporating phase-change mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods (red). The 2008 Robeson upper bound is plotted as a grey line.\(^{24}\)

The presence of diamines in these phase-change MOF fillers could influence \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) transport through several mechanisms, and the results presented here lend some insight into the relative impact of each. With diamines present in the MOF channel, the free diffusion of both gasses is restricted due to the decrease in free volume relative to the bare framework. On the other hand, with diamines present, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is chemisorbed strongly and selectively through the cooperative formation of ammonium carbamate chains; weak physisorption of either gas is still possible. Comparing the pressures used in the permeability measurements to the room-temperature \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm for a sample of mmen-\(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpdc})\) nanorods suggests that ammonium carbamate formation should be highly favored throughout the membrane, since even the lower downstream pressure rapidly exceeds the 1 mbar adsorption threshold during the measurement. Because \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) saturation is measured at equilibrium, an increase in solubility, rather than diffusivity, more likely explains the increase in permeability. A detectable increase in solubility during steady-state permeability measurements suggests fast exchange between carbamate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and mobile \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in the pores. To our knowledge, the kinetics of this exchange is not yet well understood, and our results thus motivate further study of the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) dynamics in this system. A classical model of facilitated transport\(^{25}\) captures some aspects of this system, but does not adequately describe the cooperative uptake. As \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) does not participate in cooperative adsorption, its permeability is less dependent on the MOF chemical functionality and generally
